# Explore the Boundary–Tropical Blueprint

A degeneration of a curve can be read in two languages: intersecting boundary divisors and rays of a tropical cone. Begin with the [introductory overview of tropical geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_geometry), then use the live explorer to select a collection of degeneration directions.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The crucial condition is not merely a one-to-one pairing of labels. Every collection must be incident on one side exactly when its image is a face on the other. The exhaustive validation algorithm makes that condition computational.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

## Rank and codimension

Because labels are transported through a bijection, a face and its image contain the same number of elements. In a normal-crossings chart this is boundary codimension; in a simplicial tropical cone it is the ray count.

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the face-poset argument</summary>
For finite faces $S$ and $T$, injectivity gives $\Phi(S)\subseteq\Phi(T)$ exactly when $S\subseteq T$. Incidence compatibility restricts $\Phi$ to the two face families, and the inverse label bijection supplies the inverse map. Hence the complete face posets are order-isomorphic; unions, intersections, and cardinality are preserved as immediate set-theoretic consequences.
</details>

## Zoom in: links of strata

A link records directions disjoint from a chosen face that can still be added compatibly. Move the control to see which directions remain around each vertex.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal why links correspond</summary>
A bijection preserves disjointness. It also sends $	au\cup\sigma$ to $\Phi(	au)\cup\Phi(\sigma)$. Therefore the two defining tests for membership in a link are preserved in both directions.
</details>

## Specialization without changing genus

For a connected weighted graph, arithmetic genus is $g=W+E+1-V$. A non-loop contraction lowers both $V$ and $E$; a loop contraction lowers $E$ and raises $W$. Either update leaves $g$ unchanged. Experiment with both operations below, or review the background on [moduli of algebraic curves](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moduli_of_algebraic_curves).

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the contraction calculation</summary>
For a non-loop, $W+(E-1)+1-(V-1)=W+E+1-V$. For a loop, $(W+1)+(E-1)+1-V=W+E+1-V$. Since the marking count $N$ also stays fixed, both $2g+N$ and $2g-2+N$ remain fixed. Induction extends the result to every finite admissible sequence.
</details>

## What the theorem does—and does not—say

The incidence comparison supplies the complete simplicial blueprint: strata, specializations, codimensions, intersections, and local links. A global toric realization requires more: compatible integral lattices and trivial monodromy. This is why the natural next steps concern stacky automorphisms, lattice gluing, recursive link decompositions, and tropical balancing weights.

{{demo:1}}

